00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.698
Heart rate, in
particular, is going

00:05:31.698 --> 00:05:33.480
to be our primary indicator.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.390
So hardware and IoT--

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.307
how do you pick what
hardware you want to use?

00:05:38.307 --> 00:05:39.931
Once you pick your
hardware, how do you

00:05:39.931 --> 00:05:42.120
pick what programming
language, what software you

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.830
want to use on that device?

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.270
There is a problem
for those of us

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.560
that are building out
these devices right now.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.760
And the reality is, who
was alive in the '70s?

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.240
That's the first question.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:52.740
OK, good.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:53.906
There's at least some of us.

00:05:53.906 --> 00:05:54.960
I don't feel too old yet.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:58.790
So in the '70s, we
had VHS and Betamax.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:00.610
Did anyone own a Betamax player?

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.910
Yes, yeah, me, too.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.740
Next if you heard
about laser discs?

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.270
Anyone hear about laser discs?

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.550
Yeah, all right, so
laser discs and DVDs.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.717
And now we've got
Blu-ray and HD.

00:06:10.717 --> 00:06:12.800
Hopefully, most of the
room would know about that.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.360
So it's the idea of standards.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.280
Each of those
technologies eventually

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.730
resolved into a standard that
most of the market could adopt.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.620
In IoT, we don't have
standards right now.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:23.835
It is a complete Wild West.

00:06:23.835 --> 00:06:25.710
You could pick any
hardware and any software,

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:26.770
and it would still work.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:29.100
This is just a small
sampling of the options

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.570
that you've got up there today.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.167
Sometimes an
environmental factor

00:06:32.167 --> 00:06:33.750
will help you narrow
down this choice.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.760
So if you're working on
the side of a mountain,

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:36.840
your options will slim down.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.256
You've got
communication problems.

00:06:38.256 --> 00:06:39.840
You've got power
consumption issues.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.960
That can help dictate what
hardware that you're picking.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.582
The reality is that for most
large scale IoT installations,

00:06:45.582 --> 00:06:47.790
you're going to have custom
hardware that gets built.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:49.373
You're going to have
a microcontroller

00:06:49.373 --> 00:06:52.050
with custom sensors
that really defines

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.540
the minimum device that
you need to accomplish,

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.010
whatever the goal is.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.680
Our only real requirement
is to read analog sensors.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.110
Our heart rate and temperature
sensors are analog.

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:02.350
They're off the shelf.

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:04.830
So we needed something
that could speak analog.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.784
We also want it
super easy to set up.

00:07:06.784 --> 00:07:08.950
We've only got 45 minutes
to build the entire thing.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:10.980
So I wanted to be sure
that whatever we picked

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:12.540
would be really easy to set up.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:15.510
The Python libraries
have everything we need.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.670
Everything that we need
to do this end to end,

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:18.960
Python has libraries for them.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:19.760
And it was out of the box.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.510
We wanted something
that could run Python.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:24.150
So our device is an
Arduino, so that we

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:26.010
could read the analog
devices really easily,

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.509
connected over
serial to a Raspberry

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:30.030
Pi, which is super easy to
connect and runs Python.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.560
Production, obviously,
for most IoT,

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:32.970
that's not what you're
going to end up using.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.060
It's not cost effective.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.710
But for our purposes, just
to highlight the technology,

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:38.130
it's going to work great.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.484
So let's take a look, if
we could, at the device

00:07:40.484 --> 00:07:41.400
that Calum's building.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.264
Could we switch to
demo one, please?

00:07:43.264 --> 00:07:45.930
What we're seeing here is we can
see the Arduino chip right here

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:47.160
on top of our device.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.500
And inside this case
is the Raspberry Pi.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.130
The two sensors that
we're seeing now

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:54.620
is the heart rate sensor,
which is on a little ear clip.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.470
And then we've got the
temperature sensor,

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:57.386
this little probe guy.

00:07:57.386 --> 00:08:00.560
So I'm going to have that
temperature sensor in my mouth.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.600
And we'll see how
accurate a reading

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:04.550
we can get as I'm
trying to give a talk

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.730
with a sensor in my mouth.

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.230
So before we check
out the software

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:09.855
that Calum's got on
the device running,

00:08:09.855 --> 00:08:13.290
can we switch back to
slides now, please.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:19.640
I want to talk to us a little
bit about ingesting our data.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:20.550
Yay.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:22.370
So before we can
ingest data, there

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:23.830
is something I
want to talk about,

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:26.910
and it's going to be the
hill that I die upon for IoT.

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:27.870
And it's a question.

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:32.570
What does the S
in IoT stand for?

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:35.059
Security-- it's funny,
because there's no S in IoT.

00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:35.690
You get that?

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:36.440
Ha ha.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:37.909
So there's a couple
of anecdotes I

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:40.070
want to tell about security
that kind of highlights

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:40.760
why you should care.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:42.301
A couple of years
ago, a bunch of you

00:08:42.301 --> 00:08:46.730
may remember, that the whole
internet, large swaths of it

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:48.590
went down for hours.

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:50.860
It was something called
the Mirai botnet attack.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:53.810
And what Mirai software did is
it scanned the whole internet

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:56.160
for unsecured IoT devices.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.970
It found 11.3 million of them.

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:00.920
It launched a
massive botnet attack

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:03.285
against DYN, the domain
services for the whole internet.

00:09:03.285 --> 00:09:05.410
And it took the internet
down for a bunch of hours.

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:06.624
So that's the macro view--

00:09:06.624 --> 00:09:08.540
all of those devices
could take down something

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:09.950
like the internet for a while.

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:13.610
Micro view-- last year, there
was a single temperature sensor

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:15.419
in a fishtank in a casino.

00:09:15.419 --> 00:09:17.210
And it was connected
to an internal machine

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:20.270
so it could regulate the
temperature of that fish tank.

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.880
And an enterprising
young Finnish hacker

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.614
found that temperature
sensor, hacked it,

00:09:24.614 --> 00:09:26.030
got access to the
internal machine

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:27.988
and pulled out 10 gigabytes
of high roller data

00:09:27.988 --> 00:09:29.120
from that casino.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:31.430
They lost millions of
dollars and lost face

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:32.750
to all those high rollers.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:34.714
So this is why you need
to care about security.

00:09:34.714 --> 00:09:37.130
The macro view with the Mirai
botnet attack and the micro,

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:39.800
with this Finnish hacker
taking all of the casino data.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:41.930
I could talk for a
long time about how

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:44.030
you can counter-measure
this, how

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:45.720
you can secure your devices.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:48.380
I'm not going to because,
on Thursday at 10:20,

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.360
there is Security and IoT Talk.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.730
It's being done by a colleague
and a partner of ours.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:54.180
That is a really good talk.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:57.740
So if you want to learn and go
into depth on security and IoT,

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.560
go to their talk, because
it's going to be really good.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:02.270
For ingestion, then--
back to that-- this

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:04.880
is why I picked Cloud IoT
Core as our choice for that.

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:08.450
Cloud IoT Core is the Google
Cloud Platform gateway

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:10.430
for devices to send
their telemetry data up

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:11.360
into the Cloud.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:14.397
It handles things like
device authorization,

00:10:14.397 --> 00:10:15.980
that security piece
I'm talking about.

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:18.265
Management-- it allows you
to configure your devices

00:10:18.265 --> 00:10:18.890
from the Cloud.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:21.951
You can send messages
back down to your devices.

00:10:21.951 --> 00:10:24.200
It allows you to have your
devices report their state.

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:26.540
So you can check the health
of your fleet from the Cloud

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:30.022
without having to talk
directly to your device.

00:10:30.022 --> 00:10:30.980
It's a managed service.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:32.604
It's just an endpoint
that you talk to.

00:10:32.604 --> 00:10:34.820
So you configure your
devices to talk to a URL.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:36.030
There's no client library.

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:37.446
So if space is an
issue, if you're

00:10:37.446 --> 00:10:39.831
on a microcontroller
with only 200k of space,

00:10:39.831 --> 00:10:40.580
it's at a premium.

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.210
You don't want to
have client libraries.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:43.459
IoT Core lets you handle that.

00:10:45.711 --> 00:10:46.210
All right.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:51.310
So before I show you
the code that Calum

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:52.977
is writing on the
device, I want to talk

00:10:52.977 --> 00:10:55.226
to you about what the other
options that you could do.

00:10:55.226 --> 00:10:56.770
Instead of talking
to IoT Core, what

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:58.854
are some other possibilities
that you could do?

00:10:58.854 --> 00:11:00.520
You can leave IoT
Core and talk directly

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:03.670
to the event queue system
that backs it, Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:05.020
So you could cut out IoT Core.

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:07.120
I left the management
block on here on purpose.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:09.670
Because if you skip IoT
Core, what you're left with

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:11.120
is having to manage
your devices.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:12.310
You have to authorize them.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.014
You have to deal with them if
one of them gets compromised

00:11:15.014 --> 00:11:16.180
and you have to shut it off.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:19.530
You have to now manage all
of that yourself in software.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.370
Another option-- you
could skip all of that

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:23.900
and talk directly
to your data store.

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:26.626
Now on top of having to
manage all your devices,

00:11:26.626 --> 00:11:28.000
you also have this
problem where,

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.250
what if your right to
your data store fails?

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:32.530
Someone kicks a cable
or sunspots or just

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:34.104
a plain networking problem?

00:11:34.104 --> 00:11:36.520
Now all of that retry code to
get your data into your data

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:39.220
warehouse, instead of already
having your data in the Cloud,

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:41.650
in something safe
like Pub/Sub, now you

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:43.540
have to redo it
from your device.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.712
Which, for devices, network
is your battery killer.

00:11:45.712 --> 00:11:47.920
If you have to keep running
network from your device,

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:49.180
that's going to run
you out of batteries,

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:50.762
and causes this
cascade of failure.

00:11:50.762 --> 00:11:53.345
So if you skip all of this and
talking directly to data store,

00:11:53.345 --> 00:11:55.300
your devices or an
edge machine is now

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:58.300
responsible for doing
all of that retry logic.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.760
So again, this is why
we went with IoT Core.

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:02.500
It's the way to go.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.670
So we're almost ready
to talk to that code.

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:05.170
No, I lied.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:07.830
We actually are ready to
talk the code from Calum now.

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:11.889
So if now we could switch
to the demo one, awesome.

00:12:11.889 --> 00:12:14.180
And Calum is actually going
to take us through the code

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:15.566
that he wrote on the device.

00:12:15.566 --> 00:12:16.690
CALUM BARNES: Thanks, Gabe.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:20.350
So what I've done, since we
only have 45 minutes here,

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:22.480
is basically copied
most of the code

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:25.480
from the IoT Core
Python tutorial

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:29.060
written by another great
dev advocate, Gus Glass.

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:32.470
And what that does for us is
make two things really easy--

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:34.420
connecting and
authenticating with IoT Core,

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:37.432
and then sending messages
to IoT Core over MQTT.

00:12:37.432 --> 00:12:38.890
And those are the
messages that are

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:40.431
going to contain
the data we're going

00:12:40.431 --> 00:12:42.010
to build our application with.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:44.200
So you can see in this
Python sample that

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:48.610
comes straight from the website,
we have a Create Jot function.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:51.490
If you go to that security talk
Gabe mentioned on Thursday,

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:54.310
you'll find out even more about
how IoT authentication works.

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:57.280
But we create a special
Jot authentication token

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:59.444
based on a private
key on the device.

00:12:59.444 --> 00:13:00.860
And this function
makes it really,

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:04.064
really easy to generate that
and send it to IoT Core.

00:13:04.064 --> 00:13:05.980
And then the rest of
what we have is basically

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:10.720
boilerplate Paho MQTT code to
connect to the IoT Core MQTT

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:12.190
front ends.

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:14.170
All I've added in here
in the last few minutes

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:16.240
is some configuration
information

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.430
about the specific
project that Martin set up

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:20.410
when we got started.

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:24.790
And then a little bit of code
to talk to the serial input

00:13:24.790 --> 00:13:27.220
from the Arduino
and toggle to LED

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:30.590
when we get over some limits
that we'll talk about later.

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:33.400
So all of this has been
pretty easy to write,

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.850
because I was able to
leverage the samples that we

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:39.590
have on the IoT Core
documentation website.

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:40.340
GABE WEISS: Great.

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:41.465
CALUM BARNES: Thanks, Gabe.

00:13:41.465 --> 00:13:42.740
GABE WEISS: Thanks, Calum.

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:44.573
So we've now covered
kind of the why and the

00:13:44.573 --> 00:13:47.464
how of devices and ingestion
in the Cloud getting up there.

00:13:47.464 --> 00:13:49.130
Next up, we need to
get all of that data

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:51.800
from Cloud Pub/Sub,
where IoT Core puts it,

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:53.870
into our data storage.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:57.140
We've decided to use Google
Cloud Functions to do that.

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:59.090
Cloud Functions are
serverless methods.

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:02.540
They can be called with either
an HTTP GET or POST call

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:05.431
or triggered from something
that happens in Google Cloud

00:14:05.431 --> 00:14:05.930
Platform.

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:10.370
For us, we're using
Pub/Sub as our trigger.

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:12.520
So any message that
comes into Cloud Pub/Sub

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:14.942
will fire off this function.

00:14:14.942 --> 00:14:17.150
Since I know Martin's probably
already getting bored,

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:19.154
since he finished this
in the first two minutes,

00:14:19.154 --> 00:14:20.570
we're going to cut
over to Martin,

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:22.970
and we're going to show some
of the code that he did.

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:26.871
So we need to switch
to demo two, please.

00:14:26.871 --> 00:14:28.120
MARTIN VARELA: Yep, that's it.

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:28.380
GABE WEISS: Awesome.

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:29.720
MARTIN VARELA: Thank you, Gabe.

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:32.000
So I just finished
five minutes ago--

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:33.740
I didn't have that much time--

00:14:33.740 --> 00:14:37.510
deploying this function that is
called telemetry to Firestore.

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:40.160
Again, as Gabe said,
what this is doing,

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:43.550
this function is subscribed
to Pub/Sub topic,

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:46.760
so that every time one of the
devices that we are building

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:50.810
sends telemetry to IoT Core,
that sends a pops up message

00:14:50.810 --> 00:14:54.830
and triggers a new
execution or a new instance

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:56.340
of this function.

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:59.420
Let's take a look
at the code here.

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:02.330
As you can see, this is
written in JavaScript.

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:06.130
And it's running on a
Node.js V6 environment.

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:09.350
At the top, I'm just calling
and importing the library

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:14.330
that I'm using to
write to Firestore

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:17.230
and initializing the app.

00:15:17.230 --> 00:15:20.206
Because we are running on
this managed environment,

00:15:20.206 --> 00:15:21.830
we don't need to do
any authentication.

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:23.390
That's handled for us.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:28.190
And then the about 20
lines that are next

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:30.120
is the output function.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:33.620
The function expects
an event from Pub/Sub.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:35.310
I will check if it has data.

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:37.220
Otherwise, I'm going
to throw an error.

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:40.210
Then I'm going to [? base ?]
64 decode the payload,

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:42.750
parse it into a
JavaScript object,

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.120
so it's easier to
[? run ?] the attributes.

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:49.710
And then this is where I
run right to the [? DV. ?]

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:53.060
How Firebase works, or
how Firestore works is

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:56.490
that it stores a
collection of documents.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:16:00.750
And each document can have a
bunch of different attributes.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:03.350
In this case, we're sending
the timestamp, temperature,

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:06.060
and BPM, as you can see here.

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:13.210
And this is the path to where
I want to write the documents.

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:14.560
And then it's just--

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:17.140
whether it fails or not, I'm
going to console.log it just

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:18.337
for reference.

00:16:18.337 --> 00:16:19.420
And that's pretty much it.

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:19.930
GABE WEISS: Sweet.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:20.554
Thanks, Martin.

00:16:20.554 --> 00:16:23.890
So this is the glue that
joins our event messaging

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:26.500
system as the telemetry's
coming in to our warehouse.

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:29.717
Can we switch back to
slides again, please?

00:16:29.717 --> 00:16:31.550
So let's chat a bit
about the other options,

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:32.900
if you didn't want to
use Cloud Functions.

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:33.890
And there's a
reason you might not

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:35.270
want to use Cloud Functions.

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:37.790
For IoT, there's really
three really good options

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:39.530
in Google Cloud
Platform for this--

00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:42.909
Cloud Functions, Cloud
Dataflow, and Cloud Dataproc.

00:16:42.909 --> 00:16:44.450
Cloud Functions I've
already covered.

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:45.241
They're serverless.

00:16:45.241 --> 00:16:47.510
It's a low barrier to entry,
because it's JavaScript,

00:16:47.510 --> 00:16:50.090
so it's easy to stand
up and get going.

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:52.760
And it fires once per
message coming in.

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:54.886
That once per message coming
in-- that's the catch.

00:16:54.886 --> 00:16:56.385
That's the reason
you might not want

00:16:56.385 --> 00:16:57.661
to be using Cloud Functions.

00:16:57.661 --> 00:16:59.660
If you're managing a fleet
of a million devices,

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:00.950
and those million
devices are sending

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:02.660
a message once a
second, that's now

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:05.780
a million firings per second
of this Cloud Function.

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:07.910
Part of their cost is
number of invocations.

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:10.670
So if you've got a system that
has a very high throughput,

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:12.420
that's why you might
want to look at Cloud

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:14.060
Dataflow, and Dataproc.

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:17.480
So Cloud Dataflow and Dataproc
are both managed service.

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:19.430
They're very similar
in what they can do.

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:24.470
Dataflow is our managed service
for transforming incoming batch

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:25.640
for streaming data.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:29.000
It can do pipeline development
with the Apache Beam SDK.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:30.757
And it handles very
high throughput.

00:17:30.757 --> 00:17:32.090
That's what it's designed to do.

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:33.680
Dataproc is very
similar to that.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:35.630
It also handles a
very high throughput.

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:37.520
It does a lot of
the same transforms.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:40.220
It's hosted Apache
Hadoop and Apache Spark.

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:42.760
So really, why would you
pick one versus the other

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:45.030
is the platform that
you're familiar with.

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.996
So how do when you should
use one versus the other?

00:17:47.996 --> 00:17:49.370
The answer is, it
really depends.

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:51.510
If you're not doing anything
but shuttling data from point A

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:53.270
to point B, you're just taking
it from the event system

00:17:53.270 --> 00:17:54.932
and pushing it into
your data store,

00:17:54.932 --> 00:17:56.390
it really doesn't
matter that much.

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:59.120
They're both going to
perform really well for you.

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:01.890
The invocations per second
is what I'm talking about.

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:03.701
So if you've got
very high throughput,

00:18:03.701 --> 00:18:05.450
that's where you would
want to switch over

00:18:05.450 --> 00:18:06.620
to Dataflow or Dataproc.

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:08.600
For me, general rule
of thumb, always

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:09.992
prototype with Cloud Functions.

00:18:09.992 --> 00:18:11.450
Get your entire
end to end working.

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:12.658
Find out what you don't know.

00:18:12.658 --> 00:18:13.850
Get everything functional.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:16.340
And then when the cost
starts to become an issue,

00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:18.780
cut over to Dataflow
or Dataproc.

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:19.280
All right.

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:20.330
So we've got ingest.

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:21.210
We've got devices.

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:22.640
We've got pipeline.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:23.930
Next up, we're onto storage.

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:26.990
Everyone still
doing OK out there?

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:29.869
Ah, so I know I'm throwing a lot
of stuff at you very quickly.

00:18:29.869 --> 00:18:31.910
I wish I could tell you
it's going to get better.

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:32.450
It's not.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:34.616
I'm going to continue to
throw a lot of stuff at you

00:18:34.616 --> 00:18:35.420
very quickly.

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:37.146
Because next up is storage.

00:18:37.146 --> 00:18:38.270
And there's a lot to cover.

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:41.450
So for our application,
we picked Cloud Firestore.

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:42.690
It's a NoSQL database.

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:46.306
It's very low latency
and low response time.

00:18:46.306 --> 00:18:48.680
It's perfect for doing the
real time visualization that I

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:49.970
talked about at the beginning.

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:51.590
Because, built into
it in native mode,

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:53.990
it's got a push mechanism,
an update mechanism,

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:56.990
where anytime you get a data
change, it fires a callback.

00:18:56.990 --> 00:18:59.990
It's one of the unique
pieces of Firestore

00:18:59.990 --> 00:19:01.879
in our kind of storage
options, which is why

00:19:01.879 --> 00:19:03.170
we've decided to pick it today.

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:05.570
It's also really
easy to stand up.

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:07.280
So what are some
options if you didn't

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:09.470
want to use Cloud Firestore?

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:12.299
We have a flowchart for that.

00:19:12.299 --> 00:19:13.340
No need to memorize this.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:14.548
It's actually on our website.

00:19:14.548 --> 00:19:17.570
It's at cloud.google.com/storage
options.

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:18.540
Memorize that one.

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:20.600
So this flowchart
is not intended

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:23.590
to give you the be all, end
all option for your storage.

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:26.090
There's a lot of edge cases
that are little gotchas in here.

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:28.173
But this flowchart will
help you with things like,

00:19:28.173 --> 00:19:29.780
is your data structured or not?

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:31.970
Are you doing analytics
or real time analysis?

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:33.740
Do you need mobile SDKs?

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:35.930
That's what this flowchart
will help you with.

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:37.010
Cost is the last piece.

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:38.635
The flowchart won't
help you with cost.

00:19:38.635 --> 00:19:41.480
But it's so dependent on
your individual applications.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:42.890
We have tools
online that you can

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:45.223
put in things like general
throughput, amount of storage

00:19:45.223 --> 00:19:45.830
you need.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:47.390
The online tools
will help guide you

00:19:47.390 --> 00:19:50.150
through what your rough
monthly cost will be,

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:52.490
depending on the
storage that you pick.

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:54.440
So there's too many on
that flowchart for us

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:55.940
to go through all
of them in detail.

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:57.585
But I want to
highlight just a few.

00:19:57.585 --> 00:19:59.960
And to speak to IoT, there's
usually two different things

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:02.720
with storage that you're
wanting to care about

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:04.062
for an IoT application.

00:20:04.062 --> 00:20:05.270
There's a real time analysis.

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:07.160
If you're doing things
like fault detection

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:11.720
or automatic inference, you want
to get a low latency response

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:13.850
or you're doing really
heavy analytics.

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:17.930
So IoT often is just a vehicle
to get data into the Cloud

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:19.040
to run analysis on.

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:20.250
Those are the two things.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:21.999
And so I put them
on this graph so

00:20:21.999 --> 00:20:24.290
that I can put our different
products on it to give you

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:26.342
just a ballpark finger
in the wind what

00:20:26.342 --> 00:20:27.800
the different
products are good at.

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:28.791
So first up, Firestore.

00:20:28.791 --> 00:20:30.290
I've already pretty
much covered it.

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:32.540
It's very, very
good at real time.

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:34.910
And it's not the
worst in analytics,

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:36.350
but it is a NoSQL database.

00:20:36.350 --> 00:20:38.450
If you're not familiar
with SQL versus NoSQL,

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:40.869
NoSQL's not the best at doing
really complicated queries.

00:20:40.869 --> 00:20:43.160
Because you have to scan a
whole bunch of rows and then

00:20:43.160 --> 00:20:44.300
parse for what you
want within it,

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:45.590
since it's unstructured data.

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:47.690
SQL is much better for
writing that kind of query,

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:49.564
where you can really
get out exactly what you

00:20:49.564 --> 00:20:50.900
want in the query.

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:52.790
So that's why Firestore
is where it is.

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:54.020
Next, we have Cloud Bigtable.

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:57.260
Bigtable is the speediest of
our speedy NoSQL databases.

00:20:57.260 --> 00:20:58.700
It's incredibly fast.

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:01.790
And it scales up to really
massive amounts of data.

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:04.640
Petabytes of data are not
a problem for Bigtable.

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:07.220
The reason it's so far on the
analytics left-hand side--

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:08.690
because it's terrible at it.

00:21:08.690 --> 00:21:10.640
The way that it is
so fast is that it

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:11.979
is incredibly bare bones.

00:21:11.979 --> 00:21:14.270
Anything that you want on
top of it, you have to build.

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:15.890
There's no tooling
built into it.

00:21:15.890 --> 00:21:18.867
So it's kind of a blank
canvas NoSQL database.

00:21:18.867 --> 00:21:20.450
We would never have
done it for today,

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:23.030
because there's way too much
tooling you'd have to build up.

00:21:23.030 --> 00:21:24.920
Next up is Cloud Datastore.

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:28.072
It's much more of an out
of the box NoSQL solution.

00:21:28.072 --> 00:21:29.780
It's got a bunch of
tooling on top of it.

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:32.270
It's got a very
well-developed querying API,

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:34.160
and it's easy to
understand and use.

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:36.856
In function, it's very
similar to Cloud Firestore.

00:21:36.856 --> 00:21:38.480
The tipping point
for us with Firestore

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:40.790
is why it's a little bit
better in the real time-- is

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:42.680
that update mechanism,
that push mechanism.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:44.846
We didn't want to have to
build something to do push

00:21:44.846 --> 00:21:46.950
to a real time front end.

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:49.190
So that's why we picked
it over Datastore.

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:51.290
Next, we have Cloud
SQL, our SQL option.

00:21:51.290 --> 00:21:53.720
It's hosted by SQL or Postgres.

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:56.300
If you need a SQL database,
this is the option for it.

00:21:56.300 --> 00:21:57.650
It's very low latency.

00:21:57.650 --> 00:21:59.970
It's better on the analytics
scale, because it's SQL.

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:01.460
So it can do more
complex queries.

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:04.310
There is a gotcha in SQL--
you should know about it--

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:06.740
which is SQL cannot
horizontally scale.

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:08.340
It can only vertically scale.

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:10.700
So if all of your data fits
happily on a single machine,

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:11.510
awesome.

00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:13.330
Use Cloud SQL if all
of your data will.

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:15.160
Now for today, I could
have used Cloud SQL.

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:16.910
There's no way our
sensors today are going

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:18.102
to overwhelm a single box.

00:22:18.102 --> 00:22:20.060
But I didn't want to
highlight a solution that,

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:22.681
for most IoT applications,
aren't going to be doable.

00:22:22.681 --> 00:22:24.680
Most IoT is going to
produce enough data that it

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:26.430
would overwhelm SQL right away.

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:29.960
And lastly, way down on the
corner, we have BigQuery.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:31.810
Does anyone know about
BigQuery already?

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:32.960
A bunch of you do.

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:34.370
If you don't, get familiar.

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:37.460
If you're doing any
kind of data analytics,

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:39.170
BigQuery is what
you want to use.

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.216
Hands down, no question
you should be using it.

00:22:41.216 --> 00:22:42.590
And if you're not
doing analytics

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:44.673
as part of your IoT
application, I would ask you--

00:22:44.673 --> 00:22:46.370
why are you not doing
analytics as part

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:47.825
of your IoT application?

00:22:47.825 --> 00:22:50.450
Because it's data, and a lot of
it, and you can get really cool

00:22:50.450 --> 00:22:51.410
stuff out of it.

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:56.180
So BigQuery is-- it's unique
in the offering in that you

00:22:56.180 --> 00:23:00.170
use SQL queries against
a NoSQL back end.

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:02.180
And it's amazing, which
means for analytics,

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:03.440
it is the best of both worlds.

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:06.860
You can use SQL statements,
and it horizontally scales.

00:23:06.860 --> 00:23:10.010
It also will scale up
to petabytes of data.

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:11.900
And you can run
queries performant

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:14.150
for the size against that data.

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:16.460
Now having said that,
it is performant,

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:18.020
but it's not real time.

00:23:18.020 --> 00:23:20.870
I would never ever build
a real time application

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:24.350
on top of BigQuery, because
it will be terrible at it.

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:27.875
If I had more time
in the session today,

00:23:27.875 --> 00:23:29.250
and in a perfect
world, you would

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:31.020
want something like this,
where that Cloud Function is

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:32.936
pulling your data out
of PubSub and putting it

00:23:32.936 --> 00:23:35.970
into your real time analysis
data store, like Firestore.

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:38.670
You would also duplicate
the data over into your data

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:39.900
warehouse, like BigQuery.

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:41.900
That way, you really get
the best of both worlds

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:44.069
for the cost of some
storage, which is cheap.

00:23:44.069 --> 00:23:45.360
You get the real time analysis.

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:47.796
You get all of the low
latency of Firestore.

00:23:47.796 --> 00:23:49.920
And you get the power and
the analytics of BigQuery

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:52.346
behind it.

00:23:52.346 --> 00:23:53.720
So I've talked a
little bit about

00:23:53.720 --> 00:23:55.370
latency, real-time processing.

00:23:55.370 --> 00:23:57.290
Something that I haven't
talked about yet,

00:23:57.290 --> 00:24:01.099
which I can't, is
computing on the edge.

00:24:01.099 --> 00:24:03.140
That's kind of all the
buzzword right now, right?

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:04.210
You want to compute on the edge.

00:24:04.210 --> 00:24:04.850
Be on the edge.

00:24:04.850 --> 00:24:07.400
I could spend an entire
talk discussing Cloud

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:09.740
versus the Edge versus
Hybrid versus Fog,

00:24:09.740 --> 00:24:11.300
although Fog really
is just edge.

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:12.425
It's just another buzzword.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:16.860
The whole talk is going
to take up all the time.

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:18.930
Broadly speaking, kind
of a finger in the wind,

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:20.400
storage-- do it in the Cloud.

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:23.880
If you need real time compute,
low latency, do it on the Edge.

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:25.770
You want to do data
visualization, probably

00:24:25.770 --> 00:24:27.120
safe doing it in the Cloud.

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:28.809
Safety checks on a
nuclear power plant?

00:24:28.809 --> 00:24:29.850
Please do it on the Edge.

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:31.590
Don't do it on the Cloud.

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:33.900
So we've covered all of
our headless stuff now.

00:24:33.900 --> 00:24:35.890
We've gone from device
up into IoT Core.

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:37.560
IoT Core bridged
into Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:39.330
Cloud Functions
pulling off a Pub/Sub,

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:41.717
dumping it into Firestore,
all of the no visual stuff.

00:24:41.717 --> 00:24:44.175
So we still have not answered
my question of who's nervous?

00:24:47.450 --> 00:24:49.924
So let's see if we can
answer those questions.

00:24:49.924 --> 00:24:51.590
I could spend a really
long time talking

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:54.380
about all of the front end
possibilities for an IoT

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:55.350
application.

00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:57.590
This is like the tiniest
of samples up there.

00:24:57.590 --> 00:25:01.299
I do not have a mountain of time
to talk about front ends of all

00:25:01.299 --> 00:25:02.340
of what your options are.

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:04.370
So I'm going to talk
about the two options

00:25:04.370 --> 00:25:07.630
