00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:12.357
She changed a value.

00:29:12.357 --> 00:29:14.440
It's happening in real
time, and we just built it.

00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:16.481
It's going from one front
end, through the Cloud,

00:29:16.481 --> 00:29:18.220
and updating in a
second front end.

00:29:18.220 --> 00:29:20.770
The next thing I want to
show off is the next tab--

00:29:20.770 --> 00:29:24.010
is the ability to configure
our devices in some fashion.

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:26.830
And this is where we really
see how this is going to go.

00:29:26.830 --> 00:29:29.530
So right now on our
devices we have a value

00:29:29.530 --> 00:29:31.220
called the heartbeat threshold.

00:29:31.220 --> 00:29:34.360
So if my heartbeat goes
above that threshold,

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:37.780
the LED that's on this
device is going to light up.

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:39.220
Right now, it's set to 999.

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:41.740
Because we knew that when
the things aren't connected,

00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:43.030
they send really high values.

00:29:43.030 --> 00:29:45.260
And we didn't want to
accidentally go off early.

00:29:45.260 --> 00:29:47.830
So now, let's go ahead
and set the threshold down

00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:51.040
to 120, which both--

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:54.100
I should be able to hit and I
shouldn't hit at the moment.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:56.320
So what I'm going
to try and do here

00:29:56.320 --> 00:29:59.350
is see if I can get
it to trigger and get

00:29:59.350 --> 00:30:01.760
this light to go on.

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:04.128
Hang in there.

00:30:04.128 --> 00:30:07.540
[MUSIC - ELTON JOHN, "I'M STILL
 STANDING"]

00:30:07.540 --> 00:30:10.250
I am really out of shape!

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:10.840
All right!

00:30:10.840 --> 00:30:13.302
So my heart-- I love the music.

00:30:13.302 --> 00:30:16.400
[LAUGHS] I love the
music choice, guys.

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:16.900
All right.

00:30:16.900 --> 00:30:18.580
So now we get to
play the game of how

00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:23.070
long does it take Gabe's heart
rate to go back below 120--

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:24.109
probably a while.

00:30:24.109 --> 00:30:25.650
So how this works
from Sheets-- we've

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:29.130
got a single cell that, when she
enters the threshold into it,

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:31.140
it iterates over
all of our devices

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:33.670
and fires off to
another Cloud Function.

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:35.670
It's a second called
function that Martin wrote.

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:40.182
And all it does is it calls
the-- shh-- the IoT Admin SDK

00:30:40.182 --> 00:30:42.390
and sends that configuration
message back down to all

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:43.661
of our devices to update them.

00:30:43.661 --> 00:30:44.160
Yes, I know.

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:46.030
I'm out of shape.

00:30:46.030 --> 00:30:48.644
So the App Script
is calling HTTP

00:30:48.644 --> 00:30:50.310
to call into that
second Cloud Function.

00:30:50.310 --> 00:30:51.220
So it's not a trigger.

00:30:51.220 --> 00:30:52.761
It's actually using
that first method

00:30:52.761 --> 00:30:54.640
that I talked about with
the Cloud Functions.

00:30:54.640 --> 00:30:56.390
The last piece that I
wanted to show off--

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:58.265
and we'll switch to
this, yeah, there we go--

00:30:58.265 --> 00:31:00.550
is this is the time slice
data I was talking about.

00:31:00.550 --> 00:31:05.010
So she is fetching the data
from the last 60 seconds or so.

00:31:05.010 --> 00:31:08.010
So that big jaggy spike, that's
me running around the room

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:09.840
because ouch.

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:12.670
So with Sheets, you get up
to two million cells of data,

00:31:12.670 --> 00:31:14.650
so what sheets is
really good at-- pulling

00:31:14.650 --> 00:31:16.030
in large amounts of data.

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:18.767
So the graphing is going to
be similar to the Angular,

00:31:18.767 --> 00:31:19.850
but I wanted to highlight.

00:31:19.850 --> 00:31:22.452
It's the time slice, not the
real time that the Angular is.

00:31:22.452 --> 00:31:23.910
So that's what we
want to highlight

00:31:23.910 --> 00:31:26.118
for Sheets is that you're
looking at historical data,

00:31:26.118 --> 00:31:27.090
not live.

00:31:27.090 --> 00:31:30.300
Down below now is-- we've got
some conditional formatting.

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:33.022
So any time my heart rate
is above the set threshold,

00:31:33.022 --> 00:31:34.980
it's being conditionally
formatted in that red.

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:38.400
So it's a quick way to get
just a quick and dirty look

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:42.330
at your data by doing some
analysis in semi-real time

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:44.920
so that you can see
what's happening.

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:49.440
So now I would like to go
through the Apps Script code,

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:50.774
if Alicia would be so kind.

00:31:50.774 --> 00:31:51.690
ALICIA WILLIAMS: Sure.

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:54.709
So I think some of
you raised your hands.

00:31:54.709 --> 00:31:56.250
But for those of
you that don't know,

00:31:56.250 --> 00:31:59.070
App Script is a scripting
language based on JavaScript

00:31:59.070 --> 00:32:01.640
with a bunch of
enhancements for G Suite

00:32:01.640 --> 00:32:04.530
to be able to automate between
different Google products

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:05.700
and services.

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:08.160
So the first line of
code I want to show you

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:10.010
is just where I query Firestore.

00:32:10.010 --> 00:32:12.650
So I'm using a
library that connects

00:32:12.650 --> 00:32:14.170
App Script to Firestore.

00:32:14.170 --> 00:32:16.140
And I'm querying for
a specific device.

00:32:16.140 --> 00:32:19.380
I had Gabe's device
up there, the most

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:21.870
recent 60 measurements.

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:25.800
So then I'm taking that
returned list of measurements,

00:32:25.800 --> 00:32:28.230
and I'm appending a new
row to the spreadsheet

00:32:28.230 --> 00:32:29.587
for each measurement.

00:32:29.587 --> 00:32:31.170
And kind of in between
there, I wanted

00:32:31.170 --> 00:32:33.810
to know what time slice
it really represented,

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:36.472
because they are sent
about once a second.

00:32:36.472 --> 00:32:38.430
But I am pulling the
first and last measurement

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:41.100
and just notating that
start and stop time.

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:43.830
And then finally, I'm adding
some conditional formatting

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:46.410
down here, creating a
conditional formatting rule.

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:49.680
So that any BPM value
over the current threshold

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:52.837
is made into a
background color of red.

00:32:52.837 --> 00:32:53.670
GABE WEISS: Awesome.

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:54.600
Thank you, Alicia.

00:32:54.600 --> 00:32:56.430
So now we've seen how
Apps Script does it,

00:32:56.430 --> 00:32:57.864
pulling and showing the data.

00:32:57.864 --> 00:32:58.530
Rob, talk to me.

00:32:58.530 --> 00:32:59.340
How are we doing on graphs?

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:00.090
ROB WORMALD: Good to go.

00:33:00.090 --> 00:33:01.006
GABE WEISS: All right.

00:33:01.006 --> 00:33:03.330
So let's switch
back over to Rob.

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:06.810
And we will see, look, it's
our heart rates, all on screen.

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:09.030
So each of us has our own color.

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:10.040
And now I want to know--

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:13.140
Alicia, go ahead and get
hooked up to your device.

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:13.990
So she's in yellow.

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:15.540
That's that squiggly,
way up high.

00:33:15.540 --> 00:33:17.875
That's the garbage data
